          %p The theory of knots is very visual. It can provide a link between the concrete and abstract. Some of the arguments are quite elementary, others very deep, and there are numerous connections with other parts of mathematics.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong knot
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+map"} smooth
            (or piecewise-affine)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/embedding"} embedding
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            into
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere"} sphere
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^3
            (one can also consider knots in other
            = succeed "s)." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-manifold"} 3-manifold
          %p
            Sometimes, higher dimensional knots are also considered. An
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-knot)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-sphere)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+2)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝕊
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}^n
          %p
            Typically, knots are considered up to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ambient+isotopy"} ambient isotopy
            (or
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+isotopy"} smooth isotopy
          %p
            Knots that are ambient isotopic are often said to have the
            %em same knot type
            or to be
            = succeed "." do
              %em in the same isotopy class
          %h2#example Example
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trefoil+knot"} trefoil knot
            is the simplest non-trivial knot. In its simplest representation, it has three crossings. It is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus+knot"} torus knot
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^3
          %div{:style => "text-align:center"}
            %div
              %svg{:height => "128.23235pt", :viewbox => "-73.88037 -64.76646 147.76074 128.23235 ", :width => "147.76074pt", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
