          %p
            Classifying knots up to isotopy is usually done using
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+invariants"} knot invariants
          %p
            It is often useful to consider the domain circle of a knot as being oriented. This is then represented by putting a directional arrow on diagrams of the knot. Such oriented knots are usually considered up to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ambient+isotopy"} ambient isotopy
            in which the isotopy is orientation preserving. This leads to the idea of
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                invertible knots
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/invertible+knot"} ?
            %span.newWikiWord
              mirror reflection
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/mirror+reflection"} ?
            of knots and thus to introduce the concept of
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                knot chirality
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/knot+chirality"} ?
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+invariant"} knot invariant
            %span.newWikiWord
              achiral knots
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/achiral+knot"} ?
            or equivalently,
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                amphicheiral
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/amphicheiral"} ?
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is
            %span.newWikiWord
              amphicheiral
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/amphicheiral"} ?
            if there exists an orientation-reversing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^3
            mapping
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            to itself.
          %h2#relevant_pages Relevant pages
          %h3#knots_links_and_other_variants Knots, Links, and other Variants
          %p The theory of knots can be extended to include various similar things:
          %ul
